This language will be based on C, but I will implement my idea 

-- Keywords? We got introduction characters:
f is natively (f)unction, but would use fn because tokenizer would be confused because it has no secondary charactor: f + (n = (f)u(n)ction, c = (f)unction (c)alling)
i is (i)nteger: i + (n = (i)nteger (n)umber, u = (i)nteger (u)nsigned, v = (i)nteger (v)oid)
r has no native meaning: r + (t = (r)e(t)urn, b = (r)ead (b)yte, w = (r)ead (w)ord, d = (r)ead (d)ouble)
w has no native meaning: w + (b = (w)rite (b)yte, w = (w)rite (w)ord, d = (w)rite (d)ouble)

-- Feature

-- I/O: since it's based on C, so, it would have to call external function via fc
-- Creating and Modifying data: variable declaration, r(b/w/d) w(b/w/d), and calculation
-- If/If-else and for-loop/while-loop: none

-- Function
well, standard programming ideas.

fn main in32:arg1 in32:arg2 > in32 (
rt 0;
)

-- Comment

/* C89 style comment */

-- Function Calling

fc function arg1 arg2 > return_var;

-- Variable declaring

/*global*/
in32 var = 0;

/*in function*/
fn main > iv (
in32 var1 = 0;
/* also, an array*/
in8 var2 : 13;
)

-- Dereference
? is a compiler-side dereference symbol
It would output variable stack location as iu32
So, it only works on local variable

-- Read/Write Memory
there will be rb and wb

rb [memory location] [output value]
wb [memory location] [input value]

rb ?var var2; /* read value from var to var2 in 1 byte */
wb ?var var2; /* write value from var2 to var in 1 byte */

-- Inc/Decrement
same idea from C

var++;
var--;

-- Calculation
Calculation starts under single equal sign

var = /*calculation happend here*/;

example
var = (12 * 2) + 2;
